{"page":560,"blocks":[{"x":0.04849,"y":0.05257,"w":0.34069,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"546 Chapter 9: First-Order Differential Equations"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.09343,"w":0.59302,"h":0.02815,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"to find the integrating factor once you have the standard form so P(x) is correctly identified. Any antiderivative of P works for Equation (3)."},{"x":0.35358,"y":0.16558,"w":0.52837,"h":0.02882,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"To solve the linear equation y′ + P(x)y = Q(x), multiply both sides by the inte- grating factor y(x) = e1 P(x) dx and integrate both sides."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.23749,"w":0.59306,"h":0.04348,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"When you integrate the product on the left-hand side in this procedure, you always obtain the product y(x)y of the integrating factor and solution function y because of the way y is defined."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.30577,"w":0.24325,"h":0.01449,"fs":0.01407,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 2 Solve the equation"},{"x":0.52622,"y":0.3276,"w":0.02482,"h":0.02244,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"xdy"},{"x":0.53561,"y":0.33211,"w":0.17419,"h":0.02522,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"dx = x2 + 3y, x 7 0."},{"x":0.0585,"y":0.37074,"w":0.1615,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"HISTORICAL BIOGRAPHY"},{"x":0.32333,"y":0.37094,"w":0.44031,"h":0.01365,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Solution First we put the equation in standard form (Example 1):"},{"x":0.0585,"y":0.38621,"w":0.13606,"h":0.02365,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Adrien Marie Legendre (1752–1833)"},{"x":0.57126,"y":0.39193,"w":0.04809,"h":0.02973,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"dy dx - 3"},{"x":0.61164,"y":0.40092,"w":0.05278,"h":0.01771,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"x y = x,"},{"x":0.32333,"y":0.42185,"w":0.194,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"so P(x) = -3>x is identified."},{"x":0.35277,"y":0.44764,"w":0.15724,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The integrating factor is"},{"x":0.4403,"y":0.46214,"w":0.18285,"h":0.02131,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t"